  "ground_truth": {
    "root_cause": "The OAuth client secret that partner-api-gateway uses to authenticate to the internal identity provider was rotated on schedule and the old secret was revoked. The new secret was in place at the identity provider, but a subset of partner-api-gateway pods - those that autoscaled up after ~13:40 from a stale config map - came up holding the pre-rotation secret. Those pods' token requests returned '401 invalid_client', failing ~30% of partner API calls. The rotation itself was fine; the failure is a config-propagation gap that left some pods on the revoked secret. Recovery required rolling the deployment to force fresh config onto all pods.",
    "evidence_path": [
      "identity-provider INFO at 14:00: scheduled client-secret rotation for partner-api-gateway completed, old secret revoked",
      "partner-api-gateway ERROR: token requests returning 401 invalid_client on some pods",
      "the affected pods all started after 13:40 with a stale config map holding the pre-rotation secret",
      "auth_failure_rate rises to ~30%, matching the fraction of stale pods",
      "auth failures clear after the deployment is rolled to force fresh config on all pods"
    ],
    "fix": "Roll the deployment to refresh config on all pods (done). Make rotation wait for confirmed propagation to 100% of consumers before revoking the old secret, keep a short window where both secrets are valid, and have pods fail startup if their config is older than the current secret generation.",
    "should_escalate": false
  }
